To create a link for every post in your website, you'll need to make some modifications to your Nginx configuration file.

First, let's create a new file for each post. We'll use a `location` block to serve these files as we would any other HTML file. We'll also add a link to the post in the `index.html` file.

Here's the modified Nginx configuration file:
```nginx
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
    server_name www.askfundu.com;
    root /usr/share/nginx/html;

    # SSL
    ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/server.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/private.key;

    # logging
    access_log /var/log/nginx/access.log combined buffer=512k flush=1m;
    error_log /var/log/nginx/error.log warn;

    # index.html fallback
    location / {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
        index index.html index.htm;
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

    # link to each post
    location ~ ^/post/(.*)$ {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html/posts;
        index $1.html;
    }

    # error page
    error_page 500 502 503 504 /50x.html;
    location = /50x.html {
        root /usr/share/nginx/html;
    }
}

# non-www, subdomains redirect
server {
    listen 443 ssl http2;
    listen [::]:443 ssl http2;
    server_name .askfundu.com;

    # SSL
    ssl_certificate /etc/nginx/server.crt;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/nginx/private.key;
    return 301 https://www.askfundu.com$request_uri;
}

server {
    listen 80;
    server_name .askfundu.com;
    return 301 https://www.askfundu.com$request_uri;
}
```
In the above configuration, I added a new `location` block that matches URLs starting with `/post/` followed by a filename (e.g., `/post/first-post`, `/post/second-post`, etc.). This block sends requests to the `posts` directory, which contains HTML files for each post.

