Here is a simple C++ program to control a hardware GPIO pin 12 on a Raspberry Pi 4B using the pigpio library.

```cpp
#include <pigpio.h>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    // Initialize GPIO library
    if (gpioInitialise() < 0) {
        std::cerr << "pigpio initialize failed" << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Set GPIO pin 12 as output
    gpioSetup(12, PWM_OUTPUT);

    while (true) {
        // Set pin 12 high (powered)
        gpioWrite(12, 1);
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(1000)); // 1 second delay

        // Set pin 12 low (unpowered)
        gpioWrite(12, 0);
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(1000)); // 1 second delay
    }

    // Clean up
    gpioTerminate();

    return 0;
}
```

In this program we first check if the pigpio library can be initialized. If not, the program ends. Then we set GPIO pin 12 as an output pin using `gpioSetup`. The `gpioWrite` function is used to set the pin high (1) and low (0), which can be used to power a device. The program then enters an infinite loop where it continuously switches the pin on and off with a 1 second delay in between.

Remember to install the pigpio library on your Raspberry Pi by:

```
sudo apt-get install pigpio
```

Also, make sure that the pigpio service is running:

```
sudo systemctl start pigpiod
```

And to enable it to start automatically at boot:

```
sudo systemctl enable pigpiod
```

